11:47.949 --> 11:53.690
[SPEAKER_01]: I suspect, and I might be wrong about this, that they're probably closer to bubble wrap in terms of overhead.

11:54.211 --> 12:04.574
[SPEAKER_01]: And an advantage about this is that the fact that you have, like, full control over your, like, bare metal hardware, like, unikurtles are also, like, way more portable and are also, like, way more deployable on an edge environment.

12:05.139 --> 12:07.962
[SPEAKER_01]: than a bubble wrap system, which is another reason that it's nice.

12:07.982 --> 12:17.752
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you also have virtual machines on top, but I don't really think, assuming you're running Linux on a machine, that there's essentially not a lot that you gain from going full virtual machine to go into just individual containers on an HBase system.

12:18.670 --> 12:26.716
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, the only thing you get in a virtual machine thing running on a hypervisor is you can go from unicurls that are very small to having a full machine.

12:26.776 --> 12:28.017
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, operations, they're very big.

12:28.037 --> 12:38.685
[SPEAKER_01]: And I think there's also another thing, which is that the only big thing, the big benefit that you get from containers is that LLMs expect to exist inside a full Linux environment, typically.

12:39.053 --> 12:39.253
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

12:39.633 --> 12:40.594
[SPEAKER_01]: Or a full...

12:40.634 --> 12:43.956
[SPEAKER_00]: I shouldn't even say... That's the code harness, rather than...

12:44.037 --> 12:45.618
